title: 0ktapus / Twilio
page: breaches/0ktapus-twilio.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: An SMS phishing campaign compromised roughly 9,931 accounts at 130+ organizations, per Group-IB, and failed at Cloudflare, whose FIDO2 hardware keys would not answer a lookalike domain.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 130+ | organizations | in the campaign Group-IB named 0ktapus, Twilio among them
stat: 9,931 | accounts, roughly | compromised across those organizations, per Group-IB
stat: 3 | Cloudflare employees entered credentials | and the attack still failed
stat: FIDO2 | hardware keys at Cloudflare | origin-bound, so a lookalike domain cannot relay them
Twilio's compromise reached downstream customers, including Signal. Twilio's response: hardware keys for all employees.
notes:
Start with the four figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind them.

A campaign Group-IB named 0ktapus sent SMS messages to employees at scores of companies, impersonating internal IT and pointing at convincing Okta login pages. Group-IB's investigation put it at more than 130 organizations and roughly 9,931 accounts. Keep the words more than and roughly when you say these numbers, and say whose count they are. Twilio was among those organizations, and its compromise then reached downstream customers, including Signal.

Cloudflare received the same kind of messages, around the same time, from the same actor. Three employees entered their credentials into the phishing page, and the attack still failed, because Cloudflare required FIDO2 hardware security keys. The page says a hardware key will not authenticate to a domain it was not registered to, no matter how convincing the page or how cooperative the human.

Twilio's response, the page says, was hardware keys for all employees.

The page gives this chain a place on the site for a reason none of the others have: it ends in a control that worked.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: March 2022 | The campaign Group-IB named 0ktapus starts texting employees at scores of companies
event: June 29, 2022 | A brief intrusion at Twilio: an employee is voice-phished; access eradicated within 12 hours
event: No date in the page's text | The campaign reaches Twilio, then its customers, including Signal
event: Later | Twilio concludes the June intrusion was likely the same actor
Cloudflare received the same kind of messages, from the same actor, around the same time. The page's header dates the incident July 2022. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page's text gives two dates, and the timeline shows only those as event dates.

Its summary says the campaign started in March 2022. Twilio's investigation later led it to conclude that the same actor was likely also responsible for a brief incident on June 29, 2022, in which a Twilio employee was socially engineered through voice phishing, or vishing, into providing their credentials. Twilio had already identified and eradicated that access within 12 hours in June; only the link to this actor came later. Keep the word likely.

The page's header dates the incident July 2022, which is why the Twilio compromise this chain follows sits after June 29 here. The page's text does not tie July to a single event, and gives no date for the compromise itself, so the timeline does not either.

Cloudflare received the same kind of messages, from the same actor, around the same time.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the slide says the gaps between them are not to scale.

== Who reported what
layout: compare
col: Group-IB | Named the campaign 0ktapus | 130+ organizations, ~9,931 accounts | Heavily concentrated in companies using Okta
col: Cloudflare | Its account does not claim its people were smarter | Staff were phished and typed in their credentials | Keys were mandatory and issued to every employee
col: Twilio | Later concluded a brief June 29, 2022 intrusion was likely the same actor | Responded with hardware keys for all employees
Cloudflare and Twilio both published detailed accounts including indicators.
notes:
The page cites accounts from these three organizations as primary sources, and each column keeps to what the page credits to that organization.

Group-IB named the campaign 0ktapus. Its investigation put the campaign at more than 130 organizations and close to ten thousand accounts, roughly 9,931, heavily concentrated in companies using Okta, hence the name. Its analysis found the phishing kit had been used against a large list of organizations, with the target's own branding applied per victim.

The page calls Cloudflare's published account unusually useful because it does not claim its people were smarter. They were phished, they fell for it, and they typed their credentials into the attacker's page. Cloudflare also noted the hardware keys were mandatory and issued to every employee.

Twilio's investigation later led it to conclude that the same actor was likely also responsible for a brief incident on June 29, 2022, whose access Twilio had already identified and eradicated within 12 hours. The page gives Twilio's response as hardware keys for all employees.

The page's last defense makes this point: the chain is teachable only because two companies chose to be specific about being attacked.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | A text message from "IT" | T1566.003
step: 02 | Credential Access | The one-time code, relayed live | T1111
step: 03 | Impact | Twilio, then Twilio's customers | T1199
step: 04 | The Control That Held | FIDO2 keys at Cloudflare | -
Arrows follow the page's stage order. Stage 04 is the same kind of phish, around the same time, failing at Cloudflare.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's four stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, Initial Access, is the text message impersonating internal IT, with a link to a lookalike Okta sign-in page. Stage 02, Credential Access, is the phishing page capturing the username, password and one-time code, and an operator relaying them while the code was still valid. Stage 03, under the page's Impact phase, is the campaign's reach: roughly 9,931 accounts across 130+ organizations, per Group-IB, and through Twilio, its customers, including Signal.

Stage 04, The Control That Held, carries no ATT&CK technique. It is Cloudflare, where three employees entered their credentials and the attack failed anyway, because the second factor was a FIDO2 hardware key.

Read the last arrow carefully. Stage 04 comes after stage 03 only in the page's order: it is the same kind of phish, around the same time, at a company where it did not work. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Texts posing as internal IT linked to convincing fake Okta sign-in pages
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1566.003 | Phishing: Spearphishing via Service
technique: T1684.001 | Social Engineering: Impersonation
fact: Channel | SMS to employee mobile numbers
fact: Pretext | an expired password, or a schedule change
fact: Domains | company name combined with -okta or -sso
fact: Why SMS | no mail filtering or sender reputation; short links look normal
The sophistication is in the industrialization: one kit, many companies, at once.
notes:
Stage 01 is the way in. The page maps it to T1566.003, Spearphishing via Service, and T1684.001, Impersonation.

Employees received SMS messages impersonating internal IT, warning of an expired password or a schedule change, with a link to a convincing Okta sign-in page on a lookalike domain. Okta is an identity provider, the service that signs employees in to their company's applications. Phishing by text message is often called smishing.

The page says SMS was chosen deliberately. It arrives on a phone rather than in a mail client with a decade of filtering behind it, it carries no sender reputation a user can check, and a shortened link looks normal there in a way it would not in email.

The registered domains combined the target company's name with terms like -okta or -sso, where SSO stands for single sign-on. The pages were faithful copies of the real Okta login. Group-IB's analysis found the phishing kit had been used against a large list of organizations, with the target's own branding applied per victim.

The page's own verdict: nothing here is technically advanced. The sophistication is in the industrialization, one kit, many companies, all hitting at once.

The next slide explains the two ATT&CK techniques in general terms.

== What spearphishing via service and impersonation mean
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1566.003 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/003/
credit: Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1684.001 (attack.mitre.org)
T1566.003 covers spearphishing sent through third-party services rather than enterprise email. ATT&CK says those services are more likely to have a less strict security policy.

T1684.001 covers impersonating a trusted person or organization to persuade a target to act.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains two ATT&CK techniques in general, from MITRE's own pages.

ATT&CK describes spearphishing via service as a variant of spearphishing that uses third-party services rather than enterprise email channels. It calls all forms of spearphishing electronically delivered social engineering targeted at a specific individual, company or industry, and says these services are more likely to have a less strict security policy than an enterprise. ATT&CK's own examples are social media services and personal webmail.

T1684.001, Impersonation, sits under Social Engineering. ATT&CK describes adversaries impersonating a trusted person or organization to persuade and trick a target into performing some action on their behalf, for example a known sender such as an executive, a colleague or a third-party vendor. It adds that established trust can then be leveraged to reach an adversary's goals, possibly against multiple victims.

Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1684.001: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1684/001/

== An operator relayed the captured one-time code while it was still valid
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1111 | Multi-Factor Authentication Interception
fact: Captured | username, password and the live one-time code
fact: Mechanism | relayed to the genuine login inside the code's validity window
fact: Victim sees | a login that appears to work
fact: Factors defeated | SMS codes, TOTP app codes, push approval
"We have MFA" is not the end of the sentence: every phishable factor fails here.
notes:
The page calls this the step that matters. It maps it to T1111, Multi-Factor Authentication Interception.

The phishing page captured the username, the password and the one-time code together, and an operator relayed them to the real login while the code was still valid. A time-based one-time code is a shared secret with a short life, so an attacker standing between the victim and the real site can simply forward it inside its validity window. The victim sees a login that appears to succeed. The attacker holds a real session.

MFA is multi-factor authentication, and TOTP is a time-based one-time password, the kind an authenticator app shows. A push approval is a sign-in prompt the user taps to approve. The page says every phishable factor fails the same way here: SMS codes, authenticator app codes and push approvals, because all of them are things the user can be induced to hand over or approve.

The 0ktapus kit reportedly relayed to a Telegram channel so operators could act on codes immediately, which the page says is what makes the technique scale past one careful attacker. Keep the word reportedly.

The next slide explains time-based codes in general terms.

== What a time-based one-time code is
layout: background
source: RFC 6238, TOTP: Time-Based One-Time Password Algorithm | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6238
credit: Also from the OWASP Multifactor Authentication Cheat Sheet (cheatsheetseries.owasp.org)
TOTP, from RFC 6238, computes a one-time code from a shared secret and the current time step. A code exposed to a third party before use can be used by that party within the time step, the RFC notes.
notes:
Background, from RFC 6238 and OWASP, in general terms.

RFC 6238 defines TOTP as the time-based variant of the HMAC-based one-time password algorithm, HOTP. The code is computed from a shared secret and a value derived from the current time and a time step, and the prover and the verifier must use the same time step. The RFC recommends a default time step of 30 seconds, as a balance between security and usability, and warns that a larger time step exposes a larger window to attack: a code exposed to a third party before it is used can be consumed by that party within the window. The page does not say what time step the codes in this campaign used.

OWASP's Multifactor Authentication Cheat Sheet describes one-time password tokens as asking the user to submit a constantly changing numeric code, with time-based codes the most common. It lists hardware and software OTP tokens as susceptible to phishing, although short-lived, and lists codes sent by SMS as susceptible to phishing too.

Also from the OWASP Multifactor Authentication Cheat Sheet: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Multifactor_Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html

== At Twilio the access reached customer data, and through Twilio, Signal users
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Impact
technique: T1199 | Trusted Relationship
fact: Campaign scale | 130+ organizations, ~9,931 accounts, per Group-IB
fact: Signal users | ~1,900, per Signal: phone number potentially revealed as registered, or SMS code revealed
fact: Also linked | June 29, 2022 intrusion, likely the same actor, per Twilio
An identity provider chain running two levels deep: Twilio, then its customers.
notes:
Stage 03 is the campaign's reach. The page maps it to T1199, Trusted Relationship.

Group-IB's investigation put the campaign at more than 130 organizations and close to ten thousand accounts, heavily concentrated in companies using Okta, hence the name.

The page calls Twilio's compromise the one with the longest tail, because Twilio provides SMS and authentication infrastructure to other companies. The actor reached data for a number of customer accounts, and through that, Signal users. For approximately 1,900 of them, Signal says, either their phone number was potentially revealed as registered to a Signal account, or the SMS verification code used to register with Signal was revealed. Keep the either-or, the word potentially and Signal's name on it. Keep the words a number of too: the page does not count Twilio's affected customers, and Signal is the one it names.

The page calls that an identity provider chain running two levels deep. An identity provider is the system that authenticates users.

Twilio's investigation later led it to conclude that the same actor was likely also responsible for a brief incident on June 29, 2022, in which an employee was voice-phished. Twilio had already eradicated that access within 12 hours; only the link to this actor came later.

The next slide draws the chain.

== Twilio's reach, as the page traces it
layout: layers
layer: Twilio | One of the 130+ organizations the campaign compromised
layer: Its customers | Data for a number of customer accounts
layer: Signal users, per Signal | ~1,900 users: either a phone number potentially revealed as registered to Signal | or the SMS verification code used to register with Signal revealed
The page calls this structure an authentication provider compromised, then its customers, and says it prefigures what the Okta support system breach would do a year later.
notes:
This slide draws the chain the page describes in stage 03: Twilio at the top, then the customer data the actor reached through it, then the Signal users reached through that.

At the top is Twilio, one of the more than 130 organizations in the campaign. The page says Twilio provides SMS and authentication infrastructure to other companies.

Below it are Twilio's customers. The actor reached data for a number of customer accounts.

At the bottom are Signal users. Through that customer data, the actor reached Signal users. For approximately 1,900 of them, Signal says, either their phone number was potentially revealed as registered to a Signal account, or the SMS verification code used to register with Signal was revealed. The two boxes are those two cases, one or the other for each user.

The arrows show the order the page gives, through Twilio and then through its customer data. The page names Signal as the downstream customer and does not name others, so this slide does not either.

The page says this two-level chain prefigures exactly what the Okta support system breach would do a year later. That breach has its own kill chain, linked at the end of this deck.

The next slide explains the ATT&CK technique behind this stage in general terms.

== What a trusted relationship means in ATT&CK
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1199 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1199/
ATT&CK's T1199 covers adversaries who breach or leverage organizations that have access to their intended victims. That access abuses an existing connection that may not be protected, or gets less scrutiny than standard ways into a network.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Trusted Relationship as adversaries breaching or otherwise leveraging organizations who have access to intended victims. Access through a trusted third party abuses an existing connection that may not be protected, or receives less scrutiny than standard mechanisms of gaining access to a network.

ATT&CK's examples are organizations that grant elevated access to outside providers so they can manage internal systems and cloud environments: IT services contractors, managed security providers and infrastructure contractors. It also describes partners or resellers holding delegated administrator permissions in Office 365 environments.

Those examples are ATT&CK's, in general. The page describes Twilio's role in its own words: a provider of SMS and authentication infrastructure to other companies, whose compromise reached data for a number of customer accounts.

== Three Cloudflare employees entered credentials, and the attack failed anyway
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: The Control That Held
fact: What happened | the same kind of SMS; three entered their credentials
fact: Why it failed | FIDO2 keys perform an origin-bound cryptographic challenge
fact: The lookalike | a key enrolled for the real Okta domain will not respond
fact: Nothing to relay | no code is displayed, nothing is approved by the user
fact: Coverage | keys mandatory and issued to all employees, with no exceptions
The human failure was total and the technical control absorbed it completely.
notes:
Stage 04 is why the page carries this chain. It maps it to no ATT&CK technique.

Cloudflare employees received the same kind of SMS, and three entered their credentials into the phishing page. The page calls Cloudflare's published account unusually useful because it does not claim its people were smarter. They were phished, they fell for it, and they typed their credentials into the attacker's page.

What stopped the intrusion was that the attacker then could not complete the login. A FIDO2 security key performs a cryptographic challenge scoped to the origin it was registered against, so a key enrolled for Cloudflare's real Okta domain will not respond to a request from the lookalike cloudflare-okta.com. There is no code for the user to read out, nothing to approve, and nothing to relay.

The page calls this what a good control looks like: one that does not require the person to be having a good day. The control did not depend on anyone spotting the phish. Cloudflare also noted the keys were mandatory and issued to every employee, which matters because a control with exceptions is a control with a way in.

The next slide explains FIDO2 and phishing-resistant MFA in general terms.

== What FIDO2 keys and phishing-resistant MFA are
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, Phishing-Resistant MFA | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-phishing-resistant
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary, FIDO2 / WebAuthn / Passkeys
Phishing-resistant MFA binds the second factor cryptographically to the origin, so an attacker cannot relay or replay it. FIDO2 security keys, passkeys and smartcards qualify. Push prompts, OTP codes and SMS do not.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

The glossary defines phishing-resistant MFA as multi-factor methods that cannot be relayed or replayed by an attacker, because the second factor is cryptographically bound to the origin. Its examples are FIDO2 security keys, passkeys and smartcards. It says push prompts, OTP codes and SMS are explicitly not phishing-resistant.

It describes FIDO2 and WebAuthn as public-key authentication standards that resist phishing, and passkeys as syncable WebAuthn credentials.

Also from the csoh.org glossary, FIDO2 / WebAuthn / Passkeys: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-fido2

== Same phish, two outcomes
layout: compare
col: Elsewhere in the campaign | Employees received SMS impersonating internal IT | Credentials and live one-time codes were relayed in real time | SMS codes, TOTP and push approval all failed at more than 130 other organizations
col: At Cloudflare | Staff received the same kind of SMS; three entered credentials | The FIDO2 key would not respond to the lookalike domain | The attack failed
The page says every other kill chain on the site ends in a control that was missing, bypassed or absent. This one ends in a control that worked.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws, and the reason it gives for carrying this chain at all.

Elsewhere in the same campaign, employees received SMS messages impersonating internal IT, and their credentials and live one-time codes were relayed to the real login. The page says SMS codes, TOTP and push approval all failed in this same campaign at more than 130 other organizations.

At Cloudflare, staff received the same kind of messages, around the same time, from the same actor, and three entered their credentials. The FIDO2 key would not respond to the lookalike domain, and the attack failed.

The page draws the contrast plainly. Every other kill chain on the site ends in a control that was missing, bypassed or absent. This one ends in a control that simply worked, and it worked even though the human failure was total.

The defenses that follow are built on that contrast.

== Six defenses and the four stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04
row: Phishing-resistant MFA | 02 | 04
row: Mandatory and universal keys | 04
row: SMS not trusted for IT messages | 01
row: Alerts on lookalike domains | 01
row: Device posture as well as identity | 02
row: Assume it is broader, and share | -
No defense is tied to stage 03. Stage 04 is the control that held, and two defenses cite it. Sharing names no stage.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses beside the four stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something that stage has.

Phishing-resistant MFA answers stage 02, where SMS codes, TOTP and push approval all failed, and cites stage 04, where FIDO2 keys gave the lookalike domain no valid response. Making keys mandatory and universal cites stage 04: Cloudflare's keys covered every employee. Not treating SMS as low-risk answers stage 01: the messages worked partly because employees are conditioned to receive legitimate IT messages by text. Alerting on lookalike domains answers stage 01: the kit relied on the company name combined with -okta or -sso. Device posture answers stage 02: its heading says a stolen session should not be enough, and stage 02 is where the attacker holds a real session.

Assuming the campaign is broader than you, and sharing, names no stage. It rests on one kit hitting 130+ organizations.

Now look at the empty column. No defense on the page is tied to stage 03, where Twilio's compromise reached its customers. That gap is the second discussion question.

== Phishing-resistant MFA, with no exceptions
layout: compare
col: Phishing-resistant MFA (stages 02, 04) | FIDO2 keys and passkeys bind the credential to the origin | A lookalike domain gets no valid response, even from a cooperating user | SMS codes, TOTP and push approval all failed in this campaign
col: Mandatory and universal (stage 04) | Cloudflare's keys covered every employee | Exempting contractors, executives, service accounts or the legacy app leaves a way in | Budget spare keys and a hardware-key recovery path
The page: if you take one thing from the whole library, take this.
notes:
The page leads with two defenses. Of the first, it says this chain is the clearest evidence on the site that the recommendation is not theoretical.

Deploy phishing-resistant MFA, and understand precisely why it is different. FIDO2 keys and passkeys bind the credential to the origin, so a lookalike domain gets no valid response, which means the attack fails even when the user cooperates fully with the attacker. SMS codes, TOTP and push approval all failed in this same campaign at more than 130 other organizations. A passkey is a FIDO2 credential that can sync between a user's devices.

Make it mandatory and universal, because exceptions are the attack surface. Cloudflare's keys covered every employee. A rollout that exempts contractors, executives, service accounts or just the legacy app leaves the exact door the campaign is looking for, and the page says 0ktapus was hitting many companies simultaneously precisely to find the ones with gaps. Budget for spare keys and a hardware-key-based recovery path, so nobody needs a phishable fallback.

== One IT channel, and alerts on lookalike domains
layout: compare
col: SMS is not low-risk (stage 01) | Establish and publicise one channel for IT communications | Tell staff IT will never ask them to sign in from a link | Make reporting frictionless
col: Lookalike domains (stage 01) | The kit relied on the company name plus -okta or -sso | Certificate transparency monitoring and domain registration feeds surface these | Within hours of registration, usually before the first message is sent
The page calls domain alerting cheap, and says it buys you the initiative.
notes:
Two defenses answer stage 01.

First, do not treat SMS as a low-risk channel for workforce communications. The page says the messages worked partly because employees are conditioned to receive legitimate IT messages by text. Establish and publicise a single channel for IT communications, tell staff that IT will never ask them to sign in from a link, and make reporting frictionless. Cloudflare's employees reported it quickly, which is how the response started while the login attempts were still happening.

Second, alert on newly registered lookalike domains before the campaign starts. The kit relied on domains combining the company name with -okta or -sso. Certificate transparency monitoring and domain registration feeds surface these within hours of registration, which the page says is usually before the first message is sent. Keep the word usually. Certificate transparency logs are public records of the TLS certificates that certificate authorities issue.

The page calls this cheap, and says it buys you the initiative.

== Device posture, and sharing what you see
layout: compare
col: Device posture (stage 02) | So a stolen session is not enough | Cloudflare also used mutual TLS device certificates on internal applications | Pair phishing-resistant MFA with device-bound access for privileged systems
col: Assume it is broader than you (no stage) | One kit hit 130+ organizations | Cloudflare and Twilio published detailed accounts including indicators | Consume that reporting, and publish your own when you can
The page says the pairing survives both credential theft and session theft.
notes:
Two more defenses.

Restrict access by device posture as well as identity, so a stolen session is not enough. Device posture is the state of the machine making a request, such as whether it is a known device. Cloudflare also used mutual TLS device certificates on internal applications, meaning even a valid credential from an unknown machine did not reach anything. Mutual TLS means the client and the server both authenticate with certificates. The page says to pair phishing-resistant MFA with device-bound access for privileged systems, and that this combination survives both credential theft and session theft.

Assume the campaign is broader than you, and share. One kit hit 130+ organizations. Cloudflare and Twilio both published detailed accounts including indicators, which is how other targets found their own exposure. Consume that reporting, and publish your own when you can.

The page ends its defenses on this one: the chain is teachable only because two companies chose to be specific about being attacked.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Do workforce sign-ins use FIDO2 keys or passkeys, not codes or push approvals?
check: Does that cover contractors, executives, service accounts and legacy apps, with no exceptions?
check: Is there a hardware-key recovery path, so nobody needs a phishable fallback?
check: Do staff know IT never asks them to sign in from a link?
check: Do you alert on new domains combining your name with -okta or -sso?
check: Do privileged systems require a known device as well as a valid identity?
check: Do you read other targets' published indicators, and publish your own?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether workforce sign-ins use FIDO2 keys or passkeys, rather than SMS codes, TOTP or push approvals, which all failed in this campaign. Ask whether that coverage reaches contractors, executives, service accounts and the legacy app, and whether there are spare keys and a hardware-key recovery path so nobody needs a phishable fallback.

Ask whether staff know the single channel IT uses, know that IT will never ask them to sign in from a link, and can report a suspicious message without friction.

Ask whether anything alerts on newly registered domains that combine your company's name with -okta or -sso, through certificate transparency monitoring or domain registration feeds.

Ask whether privileged systems require a known device as well as a valid identity, so a stolen session is not enough.

Finally, ask whether you consume the detailed accounts other targets publish, and publish your own when you can.

== Who could still sign in with a code?
layout: question
Cloudflare's keys covered every employee. The page warns that a rollout with exceptions leaves the exact door the campaign is looking for.

Name one account or app in your organization that still accepts a code or a push approval.
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

Cloudflare's hardware keys were mandatory and issued to every employee, and the page says that matters because a control with exceptions is a control with a way in.

Ask each person to name one specific account, group or application in their own environment that still signs in with an SMS code, an authenticator app code or a push approval. The page's own examples of exceptions are contractors, executives, service accounts and just the legacy app.

The answer slide gives what the page says about exceptions.

== What the page says about exceptions
layout: answer
Exceptions are the attack surface.
- 0ktapus was hitting many companies at once, the page says, precisely to find the ones with gaps.
- Budget for spare keys and a hardware-key recovery path, so nobody needs a phishable fallback.
notes:
The page's answer is that exceptions are the attack surface.

A rollout that exempts contractors, executives, service accounts or just the legacy app leaves the exact door the campaign is looking for. The page says 0ktapus was hitting many companies simultaneously precisely to find the ones with gaps.

Its practical advice is to budget for spare keys and a hardware-key-based recovery path, so nobody needs a phishable fallback.

Bring the group's examples back to this. Any account that still accepts an SMS code, a TOTP code or a push approval is one the page's point applies to: those factors all failed in this same campaign at more than 130 other organizations.

== What would you add for stage 03?
layout: question
The page ties no defense to stage 03, where Twilio's compromise reached about 1,900 Signal users (a phone number potentially revealed or an SMS code revealed, per Signal).

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gap in the defense matrix.

The page offers defenses tied to stages 01, 02 and 04, and one more, sharing, that names no stage. It ties nothing to stage 03, where the actor reached data for a number of Twilio customer accounts, and through that, Signal users: for approximately 1,900 of them, Signal says, either a phone number was potentially revealed as registered to Signal, or the SMS verification code used to register was revealed.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stage 03
layout: answer
The page ties no defense to stage 03, and this deck adds none. Test any idea against stage 03: data for a number of Twilio customer accounts, then, per Signal, ~1,900 users' numbers potentially revealed as registered, or codes revealed.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 03, and this deck adds none of its own. Sharing names no stage, so the group may argue it reaches stage 03; that argument is the group's own.

Treat every proposal as the group's, and test it against the facts the page reports for stage 03. Twilio provides SMS and authentication infrastructure to other companies. The actor reached data for a number of customer accounts, and through that, Signal users: for approximately 1,900 of them, Signal says, either a phone number was potentially revealed as registered to Signal, or the SMS verification code used to register was revealed. Twilio later concluded that the same actor was likely also responsible for a brief June 29, 2022 intrusion, whose access it had eradicated within 12 hours.

The page reads this structure as an authentication provider compromised, then its customers, and says it prefigures what the Okta support system breach would do a year later. That kill chain is linked on the last slide.

